th main. Your job now is ONLY to make this branch land: merge origin/main into this branch, resolve any conflicts in favor of intent (both sides' tests must still pass), fix whatever LAND-FAILURE.txt shows failing, and re-run the gate.\"\n\nSo the definition of done: implementation complete; `just check` green from the worktree root. So I need to get `just check` green after the merge. If the corpus still uses old keywords, then either (a) I need to migrate the corpus to the new syntax as part of \"fixing whatever fails\", or (b) the tests already pass because... no.\n\nHmm, wait. But the commit message says corpus migration lands \"in a follow-up\". That suggests the previous worker intended a follow-up commit to migrate the corpus. But there's no follow-up commit on the branch. So maybe the previous worker actually did NOT complete `just check` green? Or maybe the corpus migration was supposed to be a separate issue and the branch was supposed to land without migrating corpus? But then tests fail...\n\nUnless. Hmm. Let me actually test: run `just check` on the current branch to see what happens. It'll compile the cold worktree first (give it time). And it may take a while but that's expected. Let me do it in the background while I examine the merge.\n\n.\n\nActually, let me first do the merge since that's the main task, and then iterate on tests. But before merging, I want to understand what origin/main looks like in these files, because the merge conflicts will need resolution. Let me look at the state of origin/main in the conflicting files.\n\n.\n\nActually the most efficient path: perform the merge, then look at conflicts, resolve them, then build/test/iterate.\n\n\n\nLet me start the merge now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ee5de1a0011QGn4Fpw8D0RH3","sessionID":"ses_fa11b16e3ffejSNgKGkK0vX2sJ","messageID":"msg_05ee5c7f1001Tp5Fc2pAthsskP"}}
